For a leading byte at the very tail of the chunk (i == len(data)-1): the sequence needs more bytes → truncated → carry. But which bytes should we carry? Just that one leading byte (the only one present). But wait — could there be a case where the chunk ends with a partial sequence of 2 or 3 bytes, and DecodeRune returns (RuneError, 1)? Yes: e.g. chunk ends with 0xC3 0x28 0xA9? DecodeRune would say RuneError,1 (invalid continuation)... but those 3 bytes might actually be part of... no, no. If DecodeRune returns size 1, it means the 1st byte alone constitutes an invalid/complete unit, or that the chunk simply ends there.

The key distinction: at the tail of the chunk, a leading byte could be either truncated (valid sequence, cut off) or invalid (e.g. 0xFF at the tail). 0xFF is invalid in any context → safe to treat as separator. 0xC0/0xC1 are always invalid (overlong) → safe to treat as separator. 0xC2–0xF4 could be either truncated (if it's the last byte) or invalid (if the next byte is invalid). If it's the last byte of the chunk, we can't tell yet → we must carry, so it can be re-decoded with the next chunk. If the next chunk provides invalid continuation bytes, DecodeRune will report invalid (size 1... wait, in that case it'll only consume the leading byte and the continuation bytes will remain — they'll be processed as separators, which is the correct handling).

Hmm, but there's a subtle point: if the chunk ends with 0xC3 0xA9 (a complete é, size 2, i+2 == len) → decodes normally, no carry. ✓
If the chunk ends with just 0xC3 (size 1, RuneError, last byte) → carry 1 byte. ✓
